ok let's get into audience questions for  the week one that is increasingly coming  up in different forms is this question  of Donald Trump and the federal  government versus states when it comes  to shelter-in-place stay at home orders  and shutting down versus reopening the  economy and the question is it was  received received today from mark was  hey David if Donald Trump wants to  reopen the economy before state  governors are willing to is there any  way Trump can overrule a governor stay  at home order I did a bunch of research  on this and the the the answer to me  strikes me as know from all the legal  opinions that I've read about this so  Trump can do what he wants federally he  can say we're gonna extend the  guidelines to April 30th as he did last  week over the onion Sunday press  conference and when it comes to federal  employees Trump has certain authority  when it comes to international air  travel it doesn't appear as though for  example if Trump bans travel from China  to the US it doesn't appear as though  Massachusetts governor Charlie Baker  could say no no we're gonna allow planes  from China to land at Boston's Logan  Airport doesn't seem to be the case  Trump's designation sort of rules or  supersedes whatever a local government  governor might want to do there of  course you've got the FAA regulating air  travel is a federal agency but I think  you sort of get the point governor's do  not work for the federal government nor  for Donald Trump they are administrators  of their state and we have states rights  here so if Donald Trump says everybody's  got to get back to work and Charlie  Baker here or Andrew Cuomo or Gavin  Newsom or whoever says no we're not  going to do that it might seem as though  there's a very significant conflict  there and it might sort of become a  fight now the reality is that these  shelter-in-place  orders are not really being enforced  significantly by police at this point  other than in some cases where there  have been you know big gatherings that  police broke up and arrested people for  the most part and in Massachusetts we  have experienced from the Boston  Marathon bombing when there was a  shelter in place  when the Bombers were on the loose these  are basically things that are followed  by people not at the end of a barrel of  a gun so to speak and in in that same  way I believe that there is not really  any way for the federal government to  come in and do the opposite and say  you've got to go to work now and I'm  pretty confident in Massachusetts if  Trump said let's let it rip and Charlie  Baker says let's not that it's not even  going to be an issue that people are  simply going to do what Charlie Baker  says now the most likely outcome from a  legal perspective if the Trump  administration wanted to push it imagine  that federally Trump lifts the the  guidelines and governor's say we're  gonna keep doing our thing and we're  keeping things closed the federal  government could sue a state that  happens there are lawsuits between  federal and state governments very  regularly and the litigation would take  a very very long time likely longer than  the coronavirus shutdown would last in  any of these states and in the meantime  states would remain closed there's one  other element I want to talk about the  Tenth Amendment has an  anti-commandeering interpretation which  prevents the federal government from  having local police enforce federal laws  so for example when California  decriminalizes or legalizes marijuana  it's illegal federally the federal  government doesn't tell you know Santa  Monica police to go and force the  federal illegality of marijuana they  send in the DEA or the ATF or whoever it  is that they send in federal agencies in  the same way national guards are  administered at the state who is it that  the federal government would send in to  enforce at the state level that the  federal government wants things reopened  hard to imagine so on a practical level  I believe governor's would have the  upper hand if they decide to keep things  closed on a legal level it could be a  fight it's a fight that legally could  take years and would long outlast the  actual likely durations of these stay at  home orders shelter in place  non-essential business shut down sort of  thing that's my best  now one other thing to mention I read  one take online that said now you know  the federal government could enforce  that businesses must open under the  Commerce Clause I found no serious legal  analyst that was willing to to make that  case and it strikes me as pretty bogus  but if you disagree if you believe that  there is a way that the federal  government could enforce and open  everything order even if a governor  doesn't want to let me know I don't see  it I haven't found a single credible  legal expert that sees it that way
 

